00:09:54.419 --> 00:09:56.070
- Are you kiddin'?

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:57.560
No way I'd miss it.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.940
- Not going to lie, he's pretty
good with the bedside manner

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:01.480
for being 16 years old.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:05.080
He's empathetic, he let's the
patient voice their concerns,

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:08.393
responds adequately to the
concerns, is very reassuring.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.200
Sniff is working.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:12.971
The sniff is working.

00:10:12.971 --> 00:10:15.388
(slow music)

00:10:20.922 --> 00:10:22.538
(laughing) Oh, don't do it!

00:10:22.538 --> 00:10:23.780
Don't do it!

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:25.447
Doogie, don't do it.

00:10:26.611 --> 00:10:28.897
(laughing) It's so creepy, ew.

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.500
Oh but apparently it
worked for little Doogie.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:34.660
Oh no.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:35.493
Oh no!

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:38.433
- What happened?

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:43.740
- He was asleep in his bed

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.810
when he suddenly started to fibrillate.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:48.730
- That means the heart
is pumping like this,

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:49.760
where it's not efficient

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.300
and not enough blood is leaving the heart.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.160
Essentially, the patient's in code blue

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:55.815
and they're in cardiac arrest.

00:10:55.815 --> 00:10:58.065
(slamming)

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:01.543
- I'm sorry, Dr. Howser.

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:04.250
I guess there was just nothing

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:05.740
either one of us could have done.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:07.340
- Losing a patient is really hard,

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:10.500
whether or not you're 16
years old or 36 years old.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:11.630
It never really changes.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:14.162
It's always difficult,
especially if you made a decision

00:11:14.162 --> 00:11:17.600
that then affected a
patient in a negative way.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:18.780
It's going to be a lot to deal with

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.220
as he continues in his medical career,

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:21.840
being so young and inexperienced.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:23.490
- The first time I lost a young patient,

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:24.653
I cried like a baby.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.127
As you get older, you learn to handle it.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:36.233
- I don't want to handle it.

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.780
- If you're going to be
a doctor, you'll have to.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.110
- Crying when you have a
negative patient outcome

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.460
is totally appropriate

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:47.250
and as we've gotten more
information on the matter

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:49.170
and have done research
and looked into this,

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:51.770
we find that patients actually appreciate

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:54.750
seeing doctors' emotional
reactions to these situations,

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.720
so that if you're going to cry
with a patient, that's good.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.070
Many times, 20, 30 years ago,

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:03.010
doctors would try to hide
this, go in another room,

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.980
because they viewed
themselves as unprofessional

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:06.540
if they showed their emotions.

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:08.330
But sharing an emotional moment

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.310
with a patient or the patient's family

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:11.550
is actually quite beneficial

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:13.251
in healing to everyone involved.

00:12:13.251 --> 00:12:15.918
(hopeful music)

00:12:45.403 --> 00:12:47.270
As a doctor, there's no
bigger moment in your life

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:48.790
than when you lose your first patient.

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:52.090
As a young boy, kissing your first partner

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:54.720
is also one of the biggest
moments in your life.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:58.330
So for him to go through
both in the same day

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:00.410
and he just got his driver's license,

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:03.270
it must be quite emotional
for him, to say the least.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:05.550
That's the like the biggest
factor of this show,

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:06.577
and I know I'm reviewing a show

00:13:06.577 --> 00:13:09.410
that's supposed to be
part-funny and part-serious,

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:12.060
that's what would stop or
preclude a young person

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:15.570
from becoming a doctor so
early, that emotional maturity,

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:17.370
the ability to be disciplined,

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:18.850
that when someone tells you something

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:20.930
and they're being unfair,
to be able to deal with it,

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:23.980
to have a proper level of
empathy and communication.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.990
So I don't foresee a true Doogie Howser

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:28.330
coming any time soon

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:30.380
but I will be excited to
watch more of the show.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:31.799
If you enjoyed Doogie Howser,

00:13:31.799 --> 00:13:34.920
definitely check out this
video, it's a total blast.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.620
As always, stay happy and healthy

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.252
and let's do the dance.

00:13:39.252 --> 00:13:42.419
(hypnotic EDM music)

